# Friends Like These Development Roadmap

## Purpose

This document tracks the planned development sequence for the Friends Like These rebuild.

The project is being developed incrementally so that each major layer is working before the next layer is introduced.

---

# Phase 1: Frontend Foundation

Status: In progress

The initial goal was to rebuild the original Friends Like These interface using React and TypeScript.

Completed or substantially implemented work includes:

- React project setup
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Application header
- Public scoreboard
- Team cards
- Team ranking
- Medal badges
- Registration countdown
- Registration QR interface
- Admin dashboard
- Event settings
- Team management
- Add Team
- Edit Team
- Delete Team
- Delete confirmation
- Undo deletion
- Quick score controls
- Custom score adjustments
- Score history
- Reset All Scores confirmation
- Responsive layouts

---

# Phase 2: Frontend Cleanup

Before backend integration, the frontend should be cleaned and stabilised.

Tasks include:

- Remove unused Java files from the frontend project
- Ensure non-JSX models use `.ts`
- Ensure JSX components use `.tsx`
- Review TypeScript model naming
- Run ESLint
- Run production build
- Verify no unused imports
- Verify modal behaviour
- Verify responsive behaviour
- Verify score-management behaviour

Target devices:

```text
Mobile: approximately 360px and above
Tablet
Desktop/laptop
1920 x 1080 TV
```

---

# Phase 3: Backend Project Setup

Create a separate IntelliJ project:

```text
friends-like-these-backend
```

Planned configuration:

```text
Java 21
Maven
Spring Boot
```

Initial dependencies:

- Spring Web
- Spring Data JPA
- PostgreSQL Driver
- Jakarta Validation
- Spring Security
- Lombok
- Spring Boot DevTools

JWT dependencies will be added when authentication development begins.

---

# Phase 4: PostgreSQL Setup

Configure a local PostgreSQL development database.

Initial work includes:

- Create development database
- Configure database credentials
- Configure Spring datasource
- Configure JPA
- Verify backend can connect
- Verify application starts successfully

Sensitive credentials must not be committed to GitHub.

---

# Phase 5: Team Domain

The first backend domain will be Team.

Development order:

```text
Team
  |
  v
TeamRepository
  |
  v
TeamService
  |
  v
TeamController
```

The first API milestone should provide:

```text
GET    /api/teams
POST   /api/teams
PUT    /api/teams/{id}
DELETE /api/teams/{id}
```

Required behaviour includes:

- Retrieve teams
- Add team
- Update team
- Delete team
- Validate team information
- Handle missing teams
- Prevent invalid operations

---

# Phase 6: DTOs and Validation

Once basic Team CRUD works, introduce explicit API DTOs where appropriate.

Possible classes:

```text
CreateTeamRequest
UpdateTeamRequest
TeamResponse
```

Validation should cover important inputs such as:

- Required team name
- Valid members
- Valid identifiers
- Duplicate constraints

Backend validation remains mandatory even when React also validates input.

---

# Phase 7: Exception Handling

Create centralised backend error handling.

Potential exceptions include:

```text
TeamNotFoundException
DuplicateTeamException
InvalidScoreAdjustmentException
```

Add a global exception handler so the API returns consistent error responses.

---

# Phase 8: Scoring Domain

Move score management from React-only state into Spring Boot.

Implement operations for:

- Awarding points
- Deducting points
- Custom adjustments
- Preventing negative scores
- Resetting all scores

The backend service must enforce scoring rules.

React should not be the only place where these rules exist.

---

# Phase 9: Score History Persistence

Create a persistent ScoreEvent model.

Initial information:

```text
id
teamId
amount
reason
timestamp
```

A score operation should update the team and create the corresponding score event as part of one consistent backend operation.

Score history should then survive:

- Browser refresh
- Application restart
- Different client devices

---

# Phase 10: Reset All Scores

Move Reset All Scores into the backend.

Requirements:

- Protected admin operation
- Confirmation remains in React
- Backend sets relevant team scores to zero
- Score reset activity should be auditable

The final history strategy will be decided during implementation.

---

# Phase 11: Event Settings

Move event configuration into persistent backend storage.

Initial setting:

```text
registrationDeadline
```

Potential future settings:

```text
eventName
registrationOpen
registrationUrl
eventStart
eventEnd
```

React should retrieve these settings from the API.

---

# Phase 12: Authentication

Introduce Spring Security.

Implement:

- User model
- Authentication service
- Password hashing
- Login endpoint
- JWT creation
- JWT validation
- Protected routes

Example:

```text
POST /api/auth/login
```

The backend must never store plain-text passwords.

---

# Phase 13: Authorisation

Separate public and administrative capabilities.

Public users should be able to view scoreboard information.

Administrative users should be able to:

- Add teams
- Edit teams
- Delete teams
- Adjust scores
- Reset scores
- Change event settings

Administrative API operations must be protected server-side.

---

# Phase 14: React API Integration

Replace temporary in-memory React data with backend calls.

Current:

```text
React state
```

Target:

```text
React
  |
  v
Spring Boot API
  |
  v
PostgreSQL
```

Integration work includes:

- Fetch teams
- Add teams through API
- Update teams through API
- Delete teams through API
- Adjust scores through API
- Retrieve score history
- Retrieve event settings
- Update event settings

---

# Phase 15: Loading and Error States

Once API integration begins, the frontend must handle network behaviour properly.

Add:

- Loading states
- API error messages
- Retry behaviour where appropriate
- Empty states
- Authentication failures
- Server validation messages

The interface must not assume every network request succeeds.

---

# Phase 16: Real-Time Score Synchronisation

The live event experience requires multiple devices to remain synchronised.

Example:

```text
Admin Laptop
     |
     | +20
     v
Spring Boot
     |
     v
Database
     |
     v
TV Scoreboard updates
```

The TV should not need manual refresh.

The implementation may use:

- Server-Sent Events

or:

- WebSockets

The final technology will be selected after the REST API is stable.

---

# Phase 17: Registration Integration

The original project used Google Forms and Google Sheets for registration.

The rebuilt system will revisit this integration after the core backend is stable.

Potential flow:

```text
Participant
    |
    v
Google Form
    |
    v
Google Sheets
    |
    v
Spring Boot
    |
    v
PostgreSQL
```

The exact integration design will be determined during this phase.

---

# Phase 18: Multi-Device Testing

The application must be tested across:

- TV
- Desktop
- Laptop
- Tablet
- Mobile phone

Testing must include more than visual layout.

Verify:

- Scoreboard readability
- Touch target sizes
- Modal positioning
- Scrolling
- Score controls
- Navigation
- Countdown
- Score history
- Real-time updates
- Network recovery

---

# Phase 19: Automated Testing

Add appropriate backend tests.

Potential testing layers:

```text
Unit tests
Service tests
Repository tests
Controller/API tests
```

Important business rules should be covered, especially:

- Score cannot fall below zero
- Team operations
- Score-event creation
- Authentication
- Authorisation

Frontend testing can then be expanded where it provides meaningful value.

---

# Phase 20: Docker

Containerise the backend once the application architecture is stable.

Potential development architecture:

```text
React
Spring Boot container
PostgreSQL container
```

Production architecture may differ because PostgreSQL will eventually be provided as a managed Azure service.

---

